  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "This paper examines the evolving thematic content of real estate advertising in densifying suburban peripheries, analyzing the interplay between material transformations and imaginations of post-suburban life. Using a longitudinal two-part case study, it analyzes newspaper ads from the 1950s to the early 2000s and content from 95 websites for high-density projects built between 2016 and 2025. Findings reveal enduring themes such as the prominence of nature and leisure, alongside a persistent car-oriented lifestyle, especially in more isolated projects. Projects with better accessibility also blend-in mentions of public transit and appeal to urban lifestyles, suggesting the emergence of a distinct post-suburban aesthetic. In sum, these ads participate in the (re)enchantment of suburbia by linking nostalgic rural idylls to promises of future (re)developments. These findings advance post-suburbia theory by showing how advertising resolves tensions between past, present and future suburban material conditions and imaginaries. Furthermore, unlike previous periods focused on long-term family-oriented financial planning, housing is increasingly discussed as a short-term speculative investment. Indicative of an asset optimization strategy, rentals are also being rebranded as luxury condo-living. This hints at the need to consider how representations of home and place in suburbia are shaped by financialized digital landlordism.",
