  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a longitudinal case study of a single unspecified metropolitan periphery, which may limit generalizability to regions with different suburban planning histories.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the evolution of suburban real estate marketing by examining newspaper advertisements from the 1950s to the early 2000s alongside promotional content from 95 websites for high-density housing projects built between 2016 and 2025. The analysis reveals that while traditional themes of nature, leisure, and car-oriented lifestyles persist, newer accessible projects integrate public transit and urban aesthetics, while shifting the financial narrative from long-term family planning to short-term speculative investment and luxury rentals.",
      "why": "It demonstrates how real estate marketing actively resolves the tension between nostalgic rural ideals and densifying material realities, showing that suburban housing is increasingly framed through the lens of financialized asset optimization rather than stable homeownership."
